WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.170 --> 00:00:11.924
In 1772, amateur sumo wrestlers
in present-day Niigata

00:00:12.133 --> 00:00:15.053
were performing to raise money
for a local temple

00:00:15.344 --> 00:00:18.681
when a pair of sumo pros
crashed their event.

00:00:19.474 --> 00:00:22.185
These wrestlers from the Tokyo sumo guild

00:00:22.185 --> 00:00:26.731
claimed that only their guild had the
right to charge audiences admission.

00:00:27.190 --> 00:00:30.485
But when the pros tried making off
with the ticket money,

00:00:30.485 --> 00:00:34.238
one was killed and the other
barely escaped alive.

00:00:35.323 --> 00:00:38.659
Back in Tokyo, the guild
took their case to a magistrate.

00:00:38.993 --> 00:00:41.496
They argued that because they were
the only guild

00:00:41.496 --> 00:00:45.083
that adhered to sumo’s
most “ancient practices,”

00:00:45.333 --> 00:00:48.544
no other performers
in the country were legitimate.

00:00:49.045 --> 00:00:51.339
The magistrate accepted this claim.

00:00:51.464 --> 00:00:52.548
But surprisingly,

00:00:52.548 --> 00:00:57.970
these so-called “ancient practices”
were actually recent inventions.

00:00:58.596 --> 00:01:02.225
So, what were these fabricated traditions,

00:01:02.225 --> 00:01:06.312
and how do they relate
to sumo’s true history?

00:01:07.021 --> 00:01:11.317
Our earliest reliable records of sumo
come from the 8th century,

00:01:11.359 --> 00:01:15.738
when performances were held as
entertainment for annual court banquets.

00:01:15.988 --> 00:01:19.575
During these events, wrestlers
were summoned from across Japan

00:01:19.700 --> 00:01:22.370
as a demonstration
of the emperor's influence.

00:01:22.745 --> 00:01:26.666
Initially, these wrestlers would punch,
scratch, and grab their opponents,

00:01:26.666 --> 00:01:30.336
claiming victory by forcing
their hands or knees to the ground.

00:01:30.753 --> 00:01:35.550
But eventually, matches began focusing
on grappling and tripping techniques.

00:01:35.842 --> 00:01:39.637
Over time, power shifted from the emperor
and his court

00:01:39.720 --> 00:01:42.682
to their hired soldiers, the samurai,

00:01:42.765 --> 00:01:46.519
and official court sumo
ended in 1174.

00:01:47.103 --> 00:01:52.191
However, samurai leaders still had
soldiers perform less formal sumo bouts

00:01:52.191 --> 00:01:54.610
for exercise and entertainment.

00:01:54.777 --> 00:01:57.530
Throughout imperial and samurai rule,

00:01:57.530 --> 00:02:02.577
sumo was performed across Japan for sport,
spectacle, and martial training.

00:02:02.785 --> 00:02:06.706
But in the 1400s, it took
on a new function: fundraising.

00:02:07.039 --> 00:02:09.750
Shrines and temples began
hosting performances

00:02:09.750 --> 00:02:12.253
to raise money for construction
and repairs.

00:02:12.295 --> 00:02:15.298
And since skilled performers
brought in more customers,

00:02:15.298 --> 00:02:19.010
event promoters were eager
to enlist the best wrestlers.

00:02:19.719 --> 00:02:22.972
This led performers to create
the first sumo guilds,

00:02:23.097 --> 00:02:27.310
ensuring steady employment and
improving their working conditions.

00:02:27.602 --> 00:02:30.730
Once wrestlers could reliably
make money through sumo,

00:02:30.813 --> 00:02:35.067
they became eager to perform for as
many paying customers as possible.

00:02:35.359 --> 00:02:39.572
Enterprising young men put on rowdy
street corner sumo performances,

00:02:39.572 --> 00:02:42.116
hoping to attract
the donations of passersby.

00:02:42.575 --> 00:02:45.369
Lacking the organization
of formal performances,

00:02:45.453 --> 00:02:48.664
these bouts often resulted
in fights and injuries

00:02:48.664 --> 00:02:51.417
among both participants and spectators.

00:02:51.667 --> 00:02:55.463
So it didn’t take long for
samurai authorities to crack down,

00:02:55.463 --> 00:02:59.926
periodically banning
all forms of sumo in larger cities.

00:02:59.926 --> 00:03:04.972
Wrestlers and organizers knew they needed
to make changes to overcome these bans.

00:03:05.056 --> 00:03:09.602
First, they established a boundary
to separate spectators from participants.

00:03:10.061 --> 00:03:11.896
This boundary took many forms,

00:03:11.896 --> 00:03:16.943
but over the 17th century it morphed
into a standard ring called the dohyō.

00:03:17.276 --> 00:03:21.739
This ring outlined the field of play,
introducing a pivotal new rule:

00:03:22.031 --> 00:03:27.870
now matches could also be won by pushing
one’s opponent out of the dohyō.

00:03:28.287 --> 00:03:31.332
This innovation increased
the advantage of heavier wrestlers,

00:03:31.415 --> 00:03:34.877
whose weight already helped
them topple their opponents.

00:03:35.294 --> 00:03:39.257
But sumo’s journey to become a
respectable sport really took off

00:03:39.257 --> 00:03:42.426
when promoters began
introducing rituals

00:03:42.426 --> 00:03:46.222
to associate sumo with
Japan’s ancient past.

00:03:46.681 --> 00:03:48.683
Wrestlers threw salt in the ring

00:03:48.683 --> 00:03:51.727
and rinsed their mouths
with water before matches—

00:03:51.852 --> 00:03:55.022
actions that evoked
ancient religious ceremonies.

00:03:55.564 --> 00:03:59.902
These invented traditions imbued the sport
with cultural legitimacy,

00:04:00.069 --> 00:04:03.823
convincing authorities
that sumo was above board.

00:04:04.240 --> 00:04:07.910
One notable ritual emerged in 1789,

00:04:07.910 --> 00:04:11.289
when the Tokyo guild began having
their top-ranked wrestlers

00:04:11.289 --> 00:04:16.502
perform a ring-entering ceremony
wearing a sacred rope called the yokozuna.

00:04:16.669 --> 00:04:18.796
This innovation became so popular,

00:04:18.796 --> 00:04:22.216
the shōgun himself summoned the troupe
for a performance—

00:04:22.508 --> 00:04:25.344
giving legitimacy not just
to the Tokyo guild,

00:04:25.511 --> 00:04:28.681
but specifically
to their invented traditions.

00:04:29.724 --> 00:04:32.935
After the shogunate was
overthrown in 1868,

00:04:33.060 --> 00:04:37.857
sumo was seen as a dated relic
and briefly fell out of fashion.

00:04:38.149 --> 00:04:41.569
But the sport gradually remade itself
for the modern era,

00:04:41.569 --> 00:04:46.365
and today, Japan holds six 15-day
tournaments every year,

00:04:46.449 --> 00:04:49.452
all of which are broadcast worldwide.

00:04:49.785 --> 00:04:53.122
New technology has brought
advantages like instant replays,

00:04:53.289 --> 00:04:56.876
while other innovations were
introduced to evoke the ancient past.

00:04:57.043 --> 00:04:58.919
And over the last century,

00:04:59.045 --> 00:05:01.380
the average body weight
in the upper division

00:05:01.380 --> 00:05:05.009
has increased by over 60 kilograms!

00:05:05.593 --> 00:05:07.136
But throughout its changes,

00:05:07.136 --> 00:05:11.640
sumo has sustained its status
as a beloved form of entertainment,

00:05:12.058 --> 00:05:17.188
a grand display of strength,
and an ode to national traditions—

00:05:17.563 --> 00:05:19.899
both real and invented.
